1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,687
A final scoreboard tells you who won.

2
00:00:02,687 --> 00:00:05,652
A snapshot mid-match tells
you who is pulling ahead.

3
00:00:06,053 --> 00:00:07,881
Chemistry has both numbers.

4
00:00:07,881 --> 00:00:10,236
One tells how far a reaction goes.

5
00:00:10,236 --> 00:00:12,542
One tells which way it will move.

6
00:00:12,992 --> 00:00:16,577
Aarav sits at his study table
with a test paper at night.

7
00:00:16,577 --> 00:00:19,328
The question gives him just one number, K.

8
00:00:19,678 --> 00:00:24,222
It asks which way the mixture will
move, and how much of each gas is left.

9
00:00:24,722 --> 00:00:27,984
By the end, you will answer
both with one small table.

10
00:00:28,634 --> 00:00:31,321
The equilibrium constant has three jobs.

11
00:00:31,671 --> 00:00:32,571
Job one.

12
00:00:32,344 --> 00:00:34,418
Tell us how far the reaction goes.

13
00:00:34,818 --> 00:00:35,718
Job two.

14
00:00:35,453 --> 00:00:37,623
Tell us which way a mixture will move.

15
00:00:38,023 --> 00:00:38,923
Job three.

16
00:00:38,865 --> 00:00:41,989
Help us calculate the
amounts left at equilibrium.

17
00:00:42,589 --> 00:00:46,380
Let's line up real reactions on our
molecule bench, from the smallest K

18
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:47,390
to the biggest.

19
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:51,847
Nitrogen and oxygen making
nitric oxide at room temperature.

20
00:00:51,847 --> 00:00:55,932
K is four point eight times
ten to the minus thirty one.

21
00:00:55,932 --> 00:00:57,644
Almost nothing happens.

22
00:00:58,044 --> 00:01:01,024
Hydrogen iodide at seven hundred kelvin.

23
00:01:01,024 --> 00:01:02,555
K is fifty seven.

24
00:01:02,555 --> 00:01:04,313
Plenty of both sides.

25
00:01:04,713 --> 00:01:08,292
Water from hydrogen and
oxygen at five hundred kelvin.

26
00:01:08,292 --> 00:01:11,737
K is two point four times
ten to the forty seven.

27
00:01:11,737 --> 00:01:13,213
Almost all the way.

28
00:01:13,613 --> 00:01:15,076
So, a simple rule.

29
00:01:15,076 --> 00:01:17,893
K bigger than a thousand, mostly products.

30
00:01:18,293 --> 00:01:21,169
K smaller than one
thousandth, mostly reactants.

31
00:01:21,169 --> 00:01:23,411
In between, a good amount of both.

32
00:01:24,011 --> 00:01:25,029
Now job two.

33
00:01:25,029 --> 00:01:27,779
Picture a see-saw that balances only at K.

34
00:01:28,129 --> 00:01:32,553
At any moment, we write the same
fraction with the amounts right now.

35
00:01:32,553 --> 00:01:34,833
This is the reaction quotient, Q.

36
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:38,670
If Q is smaller than K,
the reaction moves forward.

37
00:01:38,670 --> 00:01:41,095
If Q is bigger, it moves backward.

38
00:01:41,095 --> 00:01:43,873
If they are equal, it is at equilibrium.

39
00:01:44,523 --> 00:01:46,389
Let's test it, step by step.

40
00:01:46,739 --> 00:01:47,639
Step one.

41
00:01:47,544 --> 00:01:52,331
For two A going to B plus C, K c is
two times ten to the minus three.

42
00:01:53,081 --> 00:01:53,981
Step two.

43
00:01:53,838 --> 00:01:58,413
Right now, A, B and C are each three
times ten to the minus four molar.

44
00:01:59,163 --> 00:02:00,067
Step three.

45
00:02:00,067 --> 00:02:02,758
Q c equals B times C, over A squared.

46
00:02:02,758 --> 00:02:04,222
That is exactly one.

47
00:02:04,972 --> 00:02:05,872
Step four.

48
00:02:05,783 --> 00:02:07,563
One is much bigger than K.

49
00:02:07,563 --> 00:02:08,812
So Q must shrink.

50
00:02:08,812 --> 00:02:11,543
The reaction runs
backward and makes more A.

51
00:02:12,293 --> 00:02:14,718
Now job three, finding the amounts.

52
00:02:14,718 --> 00:02:17,261
We use a small table with three rows.

53
00:02:17,611 --> 00:02:18,718
Row one, Initial.

54
00:02:18,718 --> 00:02:19,873
What we start with.

55
00:02:20,273 --> 00:02:21,409
Row two, Change.

56
00:02:21,409 --> 00:02:24,691
What reacts, written with x,
using the balanced equation.

57
00:02:25,091 --> 00:02:26,706
Row three, Equilibrium.

58
00:02:26,706 --> 00:02:27,985
Start plus change.

59
00:02:28,385 --> 00:02:31,488
Then put the last row
into K, and solve for x.

60
00:02:31,488 --> 00:02:33,513
Students call it the ICE table.

61
00:02:34,113 --> 00:02:35,013
Compare.

62
00:02:34,845 --> 00:02:36,494
K is the final score.

63
00:02:36,494 --> 00:02:38,092
Q is the live score.

64
00:02:38,092 --> 00:02:40,421
K stays fixed at one temperature.

65
00:02:40,421 --> 00:02:42,915
Q keeps changing until it equals K.

66
00:02:43,315 --> 00:02:44,557
Pause and think.

67
00:02:44,557 --> 00:02:46,960
Q is ten, and K is fifty seven.

68
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,241
Which way does the reaction move?

69
00:02:52,441 --> 00:02:53,341
Forward.

70
00:02:53,122 --> 00:02:57,770
Q is smaller than K, so more products
form until Q grows to fifty seven.

71
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:00,260
One last link, to Gibbs energy.

72
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:05,899
Delta G equals delta G naught,
plus RT times the natural log of Q.

73
00:03:06,299 --> 00:03:10,066
At equilibrium, delta G
is zero and Q equals K.

74
00:03:10,066 --> 00:03:13,901
So delta G naught equals
minus RT natural log K.

75
00:03:14,301 --> 00:03:17,855
If Q is smaller than
K, delta G is negative.

76
00:03:17,855 --> 00:03:20,506
The forward reaction is spontaneous.

77
00:03:20,906 --> 00:03:24,360
If Q is bigger than K,
delta G is positive.

78
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:26,923
The backward reaction goes instead.

79
00:03:27,523 --> 00:03:28,563
Remember this.

80
00:03:28,563 --> 00:03:30,458
Q smaller than K, forward.

81
00:03:30,458 --> 00:03:31,896
Q bigger, backward.

82
00:03:32,596 --> 00:03:34,286
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:34,586 --> 00:03:39,188
Three moles of phosphorus pentachloride,
PC l five, in a one litre flask

84
00:03:39,188 --> 00:03:43,140
at three eighty kelvin, split
into PC l three and chlorine.

85
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:44,849
K c is one point eight.

86
00:03:44,849 --> 00:03:46,092
Find the amounts.

87
00:03:46,592 --> 00:03:51,423
Initial, three molar of PC l
five, and zero of the others.

88
00:03:51,423 --> 00:03:54,393
Change, minus x, plus x, plus x.

89
00:03:55,143 --> 00:03:58,037
Equilibrium, three minus x, x, and x.

90
00:03:58,037 --> 00:04:01,792
So x squared over three minus
x equals one point eight.

91
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:03,442
Solve.

92
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:07,761
x squared plus one point eight x
minus five point four equals zero.

93
00:04:07,761 --> 00:04:10,082
x is about one point five nine.

94
00:04:10,832 --> 00:04:15,307
So PC l three and chlorine are
one point five nine molar each.

95
00:04:15,307 --> 00:04:18,261
PC l five is one point four one molar.

96
00:04:18,911 --> 00:04:20,373
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:20,723 --> 00:04:22,109
Students flip the rule.

98
00:04:22,109 --> 00:04:24,070
They think a big Q means forward.

99
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:25,950
It is the opposite.

100
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:28,706
A big Q means too much product already.

101
00:04:28,706 --> 00:04:30,597
The reaction runs backward.

102
00:04:31,247 --> 00:04:32,767
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:32,767 --> 00:04:36,817
Boards ask for the direction
from Q and K, or the amounts.

104
00:04:36,817 --> 00:04:40,551
Entrance tests add a quadratic,
or link K to delta G.

105
00:04:41,151 --> 00:04:42,476
Exam level now.

106
00:04:42,476 --> 00:04:47,798
For hydrogen and iodine making hydrogen
iodide, K c is fifty seven at seven

107
00:04:47,798 --> 00:04:49,055
hundred kelvin.

108
00:04:49,055 --> 00:04:54,197
A flask has zero point one molar
hydrogen, zero point one molar iodine,

109
00:04:54,197 --> 00:04:57,363
and zero point seven
molar hydrogen iodide.

110
00:04:57,363 --> 00:04:59,272
Which way does it move?

111
00:04:59,272 --> 00:05:00,652
Pause and solve.

112
00:05:06,052 --> 00:05:07,525
The answer is B.

113
00:05:07,525 --> 00:05:10,767
Q c is forty nine, so it moves forward.

114
00:05:11,267 --> 00:05:16,363
Q c equals hydrogen iodide squared,
over hydrogen times iodine.

115
00:05:16,863 --> 00:05:20,343
Zero point seven squared
is zero point four nine.

116
00:05:20,343 --> 00:05:22,609
Divide by zero point zero one.

117
00:05:22,609 --> 00:05:23,598
Forty nine.

118
00:05:24,098 --> 00:05:27,449
Forty nine is less than
fifty seven, so forward.

119
00:05:27,449 --> 00:05:28,917
A forgot to square.

120
00:05:29,517 --> 00:05:31,057
Here is your recap.

121
00:05:31,057 --> 00:05:32,639
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:35,839 --> 00:05:38,025
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

123
00:05:38,425 --> 00:05:39,325
One.

124
00:05:38,893 --> 00:05:42,680
If K is ten to the fifty, what
does the mixture mostly hold?

125
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:44,380
Two.

126
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:45,373
Q is bigger than K.

127
00:05:45,373 --> 00:05:47,502
Which way does the reaction move?

128
00:05:48,302 --> 00:05:49,202
Three.

129
00:05:48,763 --> 00:05:50,870
What are the three rows of an ICE table?

130
00:05:51,770 --> 00:05:56,129
Next time: what happens when we push an
equilibrium, by adding gas or heating

131
00:05:56,129 --> 00:05:57,029
it?

132
00:05:56,534 --> 00:05:58,846
We find out in Le Chatelier's Principle.
